.TH CHTTPCLIENT_DO_ASYNC_STREAMING 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpclient_do_async_streaming \- submit an HTTP request for asynchronous execution, and stream the response body
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpclient.h>

ctpool_future *chttpclient_do_async_streaming(chttpcli cli,
                                               const chttp_request_t *req,
                                               chttpcli_write_fn write_fn,
                                               void *write_ctx);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
The submit works in the same way as in chttpclient_do_async and does
not block, but the client calls write_fn one or more times with chunks
of the response body as those chunks arrive over the wire, as
chttpclient_do_streaming does.

write_fn runs on one of the reactor threads of the engine: NOT on the
thread of the caller, and NOT on a thread of its own for this request.
Because the callback of chttpclient_do_streaming runs on the thread of
the caller, this path adds two hard rules. First, write_fn must not
block: it must do no blocking I/O, hold no lock for a long time, and
wait on the future of no other request, because a block there stalls
every other connection that the engine multiplexes on that reactor
thread. Second, write_fn must not call chttpclient_do_async or
chttpclient_do_async_streaming again, and must call nothing that waits
on the future of this same request, directly or indirectly. Both rules
hold for the same chttpcli and for a different chttpcli that shares the
engine. Such a call can deadlock against the reactor thread that runs
write_fn.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
cli       The client handle. It must not be CHTTPCLI_INVALID.
req       The request to run. It must not be NULL.
write_fn  The callback that gets each chunk. It must not be NULL.
write_ctx An opaque context pointer. The client gives it to write_fn
          without a change.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A future, or NULL in the same conditions as in chttpclient_do_async,
plus one more: a write_fn of NULL. After a success, the result of the
future holds ccol_http_transfer_aborted if write_fn ever gives back a
value other than len; such a value, whether above len or below len,
aborts the transfer. The result never holds ccol_msg_too_large,
because chttpclient_set_max_response_body_size has no effect on this
streaming path.
.fi
